  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1222 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nScattered showers with some embedded, isolated thunderstorms\ncontinue to move across the Mid-South with a warm front slowly\nlifting northward. While some dry air at the surface has\nprevented some of the current radar echos from reaching the\nsurface, some observations have been reporting periods of drizzle\nto light rain. Cloudiness with this system has led to\ntemperatures remaining a bit below guidance, so have adjusted\ntemperatures down a few degrees for today to account for such. A\nband of some heavier showers has set up across east AR into north\nMS where PWAT values are currently around 2.3\" right along a\nstationary boundary. This will need to be monitored into the\nafternoon for some localized flooding potential. Otherwise, we\nshould see a small break in any shower and thunderstorm activity\nin the early evening.\n\nOvernight and into Saturday, a weak shortwave will move through\nthe Mid-South with the aforementioned stationary boundary slowly\nlifting northward through the area. This will allow for scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms to develop early Saturday morning,\nlasting into the afternoon while slowly pushing southeastward.\nPWAT values are expected to climb to around 2 to 2.5\" across the\narea, which puts us well above the 90th percentile. This will\nallow for some heavy downpours with most CAM soundings showing a\nskinny CAPE profile. While a thunderstorm or two could produce\nsome stronger winds, I think the localized flooding threat will\nbe the main concern on Saturday. CAM guidance does remain spread\non where the heaviest rainfall could occur and there is still\nconsiderable uncertainty. I do think some portions of the Mid-\nSouth could see some pockets of around 2\" while others very well\ncould remain dry.\n\nBy Sunday, surface high pressure will continue to build to our\nsouth with a very hot and humid airmass funneling into the Mid-\nSouth. Dry conditions are expected through at least the middle of\nnext week, with high temperatures reaching the mid 90s on Sunday \nand likely upper 90s to near 100 degrees Monday and Tuesday. With \ndew point temperatures well into the 70s, heat indices will likely\nreach above 105 degrees for much of the area, with many reaching \nover 110 degrees. LREF probabilities of heat indices over 110 \ndegrees are generally above 50% for locations along and west of \nthe MS River as early as Sunday afternoon, lasting through Tuesday\nafternoon. Exact placement of Extreme Heat Warnings / Heat \nAdvisories is still a little too far out, but heat headlines will \ncertainly be needed by Sunday through at least Tuesday and \npossibly Wednesday for much of the Mid-South. By Wednesday, a \nback-door frontal boundary will sweep through the region, which \nwill allow heat indices to fall back a bit for portions of the \nMid-South. While things will still be above- normal temperature \nwise, heat headlines may become more sporadic to end next week. \nUncertainty remains, but prepare for dangerous heat and humidity \nthrough at least the first half of next week and begin to take \npreparedness actions now.",
